  {
   "heading": "# Epidemic curves / # Curvas epidémicas",
   "segment_index": "S1.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:17-17",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.es.qmd:17-20",
   "proposition": "The Spanish text states the chapter demonstrates two approaches to building epicurves, the first being the incidence2 package which can produce an epicurve with simple commands; the English states only that the chapter demonstrates epicurves with the ggplot2 package.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Spanish reader expects an incidence2 walkthrough in this chapter and will hunt for a section that does not exist, or will assume incidence2 is a currently recommended route the handbook endorses.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "# Epidemic curves / # Curvas epidémicas",
   "segment_index": "S1.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:27-27",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.es.qmd:28-30",
   "proposition": "The English states that incidence2 was undergoing revisions and was not stable at the time of writing, and will be re-added to the chapter when stable; the Spanish omits this warning entirely.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Spanish reader is not warned that incidence2 was unstable, so they may build production epicurve code on an API the handbook itself says was in flux.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "## Epicurves with ggplot2 / ## Curvas epidémicas con ggplot2",
   "segment_index": "S7.P1",
   "kind": "added",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:146-146",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.es.qmd:146-146",
   "proposition": "The Spanish opens the sentence with 'A diferencia de lo que ocurre con el paquete incidence2' (unlike with the incidence2 package), asserting a contrast with incidence2 that the English sentence does not make.",
   "reader_consequence": "A Spanish reader infers that incidence2 handles time aggregation and date-axis label intervals automatically, a comparative claim this chapter never demonstrates or supports.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "### Weekly epicurve example / ### Ejemplo de curva epidémica semanal",
   "segment_index": "S9.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/epicurves.qmd:280-280",
   "tr_span": "chapters/epicurves.es.qmd:281-282",
   "proposition": "The English bullet list states that closed = \"left\" is used in geom_histogram() to ensure the dates are counted in the correct bins; the Spanish bullet list omits this item (and the argument appears nowhere in the Spanish chapter, versus 15 occurrences in the English).",
   "reader_consequence": "A Spanish reader copying this weekly-epicurve template gets ggplot2's default right-closed intervals, so a case falling exactly on a Monday break is counted in the previous week's bar, silently shifting weekly counts.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "## Multi-level date labels / ## Etiquetas de fecha de varios niveles",
   "segment_index": "S29.P1",
